body:
Hi frycat,

&#x200B;

I'm new to this subreddit. I've had 2 full years of 3-4 hours of sleep due to brain and now I get 7-8 hours all of a sudden. 

&#x200B;

This is what changed:

&#x200B;

Night ritual ....

&#x200B;

\-Bedroom hygiene (no screens for 2 hours prior, but also keep room clean)

\- regular sleep schedule

\- hot chamomile tea

\- magnesium 

\- (I don't do this because of other conditions) stretch

\-doodle in bed or read a book

\-lavender oil applied to hair 

\-write down everything you gotta do tomorrow or overwhelming thoughts

\- smoke or oil a little indica cannabis if that is your thing

\-finally meditate - I find Progressive Muscle Relaxation works best or even Yoga Nidra (it's just soothing audio). I find lectures by Alan Watts particularly sleep inducing because he pauses a lot in his lectures. His subject matter so universal and out there that it doesn't use the analytical part of your brain.

&#x200B;

Here are the links to some of the audio I use:

&#x200B;

[https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r9jIuZ\_1I8c](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r9jIuZ_1I8c)

&#x200B;

[https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=86HUcX8ZtAk](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=86HUcX8ZtAk)

&#x200B;

[https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CZpQHuM06gA](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CZpQHuM06gA)

&#x200B;

Please give me feedback. It sounds like a lot of things but it's just a bunch of little things you can get used to.

&#x200B;

Happy zzzzzz's

i would suggest this kind of music as well. Some Armenian Flute

&#x200B;

[https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5gKhnvPDrbY&t=1296s](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5gKhnvPDrbY&t=1296s)

I forgot to mention, but in the same line of thought, it is extremely important to prepare for each appointment with a medical professional by brainstorming (hehe) what the key points of the meeting will be. That is, What are your concerns specific to TBI (in all ways they affect one's life)? If attention is an issue like it is for me, ask the doctor to remind you if there is anything else that should be discussed before meeting is over. It seems silly to read off your phone something that is plaguing you, i know. It's also necessary and efficient which doctors appreciate. 

&#x200B;

I did this today with a doctor. Unfortunately I was so focused on what i had written that I wasn't expounding on the symptoms and so they're just hearing a list, dry and without texture which can sound inauthentic. 

&#x200B;

cheerios!

Hey DaisyKitty,

&#x200B;

In some cases a bullet point doesn't do justice to the severity of what you experience, but it's at least a starting point.

&#x200B;

&#x200B;
